\subsection{Analysis choices made after results were known}\label{sec:S8.2}

Two noise conditions were named in the analysis configuration before any simulation: sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition, and the room field projected out. Whole-cortex results are reported in both conditions and regional results with sensor plus brain noise. No endpoint, test or margin of the original study was declared before its analyses. Three exploratory endpoints were chosen after them. The first is the dense optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) array's detectability ratio against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels in the adult. The second is $\Delta$ of the dense array against the same comparator, at top contact in the fixed helmet and in the helmet scaled with the head about the laterally centered head. It is taken against the adult placed by the same rule and reported descriptively. The third is the spike endpoint (the dense array against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm with the practical detector), chosen from << F.g4_family_per_opm_array >> comparisons per OPM array (Section~\ref{sec:S7.1}).

The regions were chosen from the epilepsy literature (Section~\ref{sec:S4.2}), and rankings among them are exploratory. Top contact, the primary placement of the fixed helmet, was chosen after the adult's whole-cortex results were known. The laterally centered placement and the helmet scaled about the laterally centered head were added after the first pediatric results were known, to remove the templates' lateral offset. The << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements bound the choice of top contact (Sections~\ref{sec:S5.1} and~\ref{sec:S5.2}). Further analyses were added after the main results were known, without changing the model. They are the check of the noise model against the measured Neuromag covariance and the sensitivity analyses (Sections~\ref{sec:S3.4}--\ref{sec:S3.6}), the MRI quality check of the school-aged children (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}) and the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Section~\ref{sec:S5.3}). Each reproduces the results it builds on to within rounding.

The pre-specified spike run was designed before it was run (main text, Sections 2.5 and 2.6) and is the only pre-specified test of the study. Pilot runs on child A, with << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs_per_band >> locations and test seeds, preceded it and prompted doubling its locations to << F.meth_confirm_locs >> (main text, Section 2.6). They do not estimate power, and their outputs were not kept; the pre-specified declaration describes << F.wr_confirm_pilot_locs_per_band >> locations as underpowered. Before the run, the pre-specified endpoint code, applied to the exploratory run's stored states, reproduced its location counts, p values and S50 ratios exactly in all << F.cf_endpoint_code_check_n_comparisons >> comparisons (<< F.wr_n_anatomies_words >> anatomies, both OPM arrays, both detectors).

The model also changed while results were known. Earlier versions took the OPM sensitive axes from the adult's stored outer-skin normals, which are nearly radial, placed the adult's head surface outside its MRI scalp (median << F.g2_conform_median_mm >> mm) and checked the OPM cell's clearance from the head differently. The final model puts every head's surface on its MRI scalp. The clearance rule therefore moves only the sites the anatomy demands, and the OPM standoff is the same (<< F.g3b_all_dense_standoff_2dp_range >> mm) above every head's own MRI scalp (the templates' scalp convention differs from the adult's; Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}). The final model takes the sensitive axes from that surface's own triangles (Section~\ref{sec:S3.1}).

This change to equal OPM standoff was made after the pediatric results were known. It lowered $\Delta$ by << F.g3b_v3_v4_delta_drop_range >> dB in the scaled adults and templates (the school-aged children exist only in the final model) and made their laterally centered scaled-helmet $\Delta$ negative. It raised the adult's projected dense ratio: << F.g2_hse_v3_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> with the earlier construction, << F.g2_hse_v4sites_v3axes_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> with the final sites and the earlier axes, and << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> in the final model. It also strengthened the adult's exploratory spike result at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm (earlier << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm >> locations favoring the OPM/Neuromag, p = << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm_p >>, S50 ratio << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm_ratio >>; final << F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_locs_opm >>/<< F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_locs_squid >> and << F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_ratio >>).

Every array change redraws the noise, because an array's channel count changes the random stream. The dense array's advantage at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm held in every version, but which deeper cells reached p < << F.wr_p_exploratory_threshold >> varied between versions (Section~\ref{sec:S7.2}). The site-matched array's deficit at << F.g4_band_45_70mm >> mm in two earlier versions (<< F.g4_earlier_v2_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm >> and << F.g4_earlier_v3_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm >> locations, the latter with p = << F.g4_earlier_v3_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm_p >>) does not reach p < << F.wr_p_exploratory_threshold >> in the final model (<< F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_locs_opm >>/<< F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_locs_squid >> locations, p = << F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_p >>). Results that depend on these choices are post hoc, the earlier results were not retained, and persistence across versions is not replication.

\subsection{Literature search and parameter provenance}\label{sec:S8.3}

The OPM studies of epilepsy, the clinical ones of Table~\ref{tab:S19} among them, were found by searching PubMed and Europe PMC on << F.lit_search_date >>. The searches combined optically pumped magnetometers, OPM-MEG or on-scalp MEG with epilepsy, interictal or epileptiform activity, seizures, children or simulation. They were complemented by author searches, by the reference lists of \citet{feys2025}, \citet{schwartz2025} and \citet{shen2026} and by a web search for preprints. For each study we extracted the design, patients, regions, OPM technology, comparator, definition of the signal-to-noise ratio and results. Of the << F.lit_studies_n >> studies (<< F.lit_studies_clinical_n >> clinical and << F.lit_studies_modelling_n|words >> modeling), the extracted values of << F.lit_studies_record_full_text_n >> were checked against the full text (version of record or accepted manuscript). Those of \citet{feys2022} were checked against the full text of its preprint. For \citet{ren2025}, whose full text could not be accessed, only the abstract was read, and its row in Table~\ref{tab:S19} rests on the abstract. The model's counterparts in that table come from the same simulations as every other result of the study.

The Neuromag noise values and the << F.meth_dewar_mm >>-mm spacing between the coils and the helmet surface come from the manufacturer's datasheet \citep{elekta_triux}. The << F.meth_prov_standoff_mm >>-mm standoff of the OPM's sensing center comes from the description of the sensors by \citet{jas2026} and from device papers \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}. The << F.meth_prov_cell_mm >>-mm cell is a modeling choice, an averaging volume rather than a device cell, whose effect on the field at its peak is at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. Four further settings are declared simulation choices without a device specification. They are the primary OPM noise of << F.meth_prov_opm_asd_primary >> fT/√Hz (the middle level of the noise sweep), the << F.meth_prov_pack_mm >>-mm minimum OPM spacing (standing for a sensor package of << F.meth_prov_package_range_mm >> mm), the << F.meth_opm_bw_corner_hz >>-Hz OPM response and the $1/f^{x}$ spectrum of the background.

The OPM noise sweep, << F.meth_prov_opm_asd_sweep >> fT/√Hz, is a sensitivity analysis. The upper end of the sweep, << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz, is not an empirically validated or clinical bound. It lies near the empty-room OPM floor of the recording of \citet{jas2026}, read from Fig. 9 of its preprint as << F.meth_prov_fig9_db_range >> dB re fT²/Hz. That corresponds to << F.meth_prov_fig9_asd_range >> fT/√Hz if the unstated reference is 1 fT²/Hz; this broadband value is not matched to the model's << F.g2_band_range_hz >> Hz band. The bar values of Fig. 8 of that preprint, the other input read from its figures, were taken from the << F.meth_prov_raster_dpi >>-dpi raster with a reading precision of about ±<< F.meth_prov_fig8_precision_pct >>.
